(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-14"] = {"chem-12-14-m01":{"idea":"A monosaccharide is a sugar that cannot be broken into smaller sugars by water.","steps":["Carbohydrates are grouped by how many sugar units they give on hydrolysis.","A monosaccharide gives none: it is already the simplest unit.","Glucose is a monosaccharide.","Maltose and sucrose give two units, and starch gives many."],"others":"Maltose and sucrose are disaccharides, so water splits each into two sugars. Starch is a polysaccharide made of a very long chain of units.","trap":"Every option text says 'cannot be hydrolysed'. Decide by the sugar's name, not by that phrase.","answer":"glucose, which cannot be hydrolysed to a simpler sugar"},"chem-12-14-m02":{"idea":"The name of a sugar tells the carbonyl group and the number of carbons.","steps":["The ending -ose means a sugar.","Hex means six carbons, and C₆H₁₂O₆ has six carbons.","Ald- means the carbonyl group is an aldehyde, –CHO.","In open-chain glucose, carbon 1 is the CHO group.","So glucose is an aldohexose."],"others":"A ketohexose has a ketone, but glucose has an aldehyde. The two pentose choices are wrong because glucose has six carbons, not five.","trap":"Ald- = aldehyde, keto- = ketone; pent = 5, hex = 6.","answer":"an aldohexose, as it has an aldehyde group and six carbons"},"chem-12-14-m03":{"idea":"Glucose and fructose are isomers: same formula, different carbonyl group.","steps":["Both have the formula C₆H₁₂O₆.","Glucose has an aldehyde at carbon 1.","Fructose has a ketone group at carbon 2.","A ketone with six carbons makes it a ketohexose."],"others":"An aldehyde at carbon 1 describes glucose, not fructose. Fructose has six carbons, so ketopentose is wrong. It is a single sugar, not a disaccharide.","trap":"Glucose = aldose, fructose = ketose.","answer":"a ketohexose, with a ketone group at carbon 2"},"chem-12-14-m04":{"idea":"Sucrose splits into two different sugars, and the mixture turns polarised light the opposite way.","steps":["Sucrose + H₂O → glucose + fructose, with dilute acid.","Sucrose rotates plane polarised light to the right (+66.5°).","Fructose rotates it left by 92°, and glucose right by 52.5°.","The mixture has a net left rotation, so the sign is inverted.","That is why the mixture is called invert sugar."],"others":"Two glucose units cannot come from sucrose, because one unit is fructose. Glucose with galactose comes from lactose, the milk sugar.","trap":"Invert sugar = glucose + fructose, not two glucose.","answer":"an equal mixture of D-glucose and D-fructose, called invert sugar"},"chem-12-14-m05":{"idea":"Maltose is a disaccharide made from one kind of sugar only.","steps":["Maltose is two α-D-glucose units joined by a glycosidic link.","Hydrolysis breaks this link by adding water.","Each half becomes a glucose molecule.","So the products are two glucose molecules."],"others":"Fructose twice is not possible for maltose. Glucose plus fructose comes from sucrose. Glucose plus galactose comes from lactose.","trap":"Memory: maltose = glucose + glucose.","answer":"two molecules of glucose"},"chem-12-14-m06":{"idea":"Lactose is built from two different sugars, one of them galactose.","steps":["Lactose is the sugar found in milk.","A galactose unit is joined to a glucose unit by a glycosidic bond.","Hydrolysis breaks that bond and gives one galactose and one glucose."],"others":"Two glucose units come from maltose, and two galactose units are not found in lactose. Glucose with fructose comes from sucrose.","trap":"Maltose: glucose + glucose. Sucrose: glucose + fructose. Lactose: glucose + galactose.","answer":"one molecule of D-glucose and one of D-galactose"},"chem-12-14-m07":{"idea":"A sugar reduces Tollens' and Fehling's only if it has a free aldehyde or a hemiacetal group.","steps":["In sucrose, C-1 of glucose is bonded to C-2 of fructose.","These two carbons are the ones that could give an aldehyde or a ketone.","Both are tied up in the glycosidic link.","So no free reducing group is left.","Sucrose is therefore a non-reducing sugar."],"others":"There is no free aldehyde hidden in sucrose. It is a disaccharide, not a polysaccharide, and it has many carbons with OH groups.","trap":"Sucrose is the standard non-reducing disaccharide.","answer":"both of the reducing groups of its units are used in the glycosidic link"},"chem-12-14-m08":{"idea":"Starch and cellulose use the same sugar, but the link between the units is different.","steps":["Both are polymers of glucose.","In starch the units are α-glucose, joined by α links.","In cellulose the units are β-glucose, joined by β links.","The different link gives a different shape and different digestion."],"others":"Neither polymer contains fructose. Swapping α and β gives the wrong way round. Both starch and cellulose do contain glucose.","trap":"Starch = α, cellulose = β. Humans digest α links only.","answer":"starch has α-glucose units, and cellulose has β-glucose units"},"chem-12-14-m09":{"idea":"Animals keep spare glucose in a branched polymer.","steps":["Plants store starch; animals store glycogen.","Glycogen is a highly branched polymer of α-glucose.","It is kept in the liver and muscles.","It breaks down to glucose when energy is needed."],"others":"Amylose is a part of starch, found in plants. Sucrose is table sugar and is not a store in animals. Cellulose is plant fibre in cell walls.","trap":"Glycogen is called animal starch.","answer":"glycogen, which is also called animal starch"},"chem-12-14-m10":{"idea":"A reducing sugar can reduce Tollens' reagent or Fehling's solution.","steps":["These reagents are mild oxidising agents.","A sugar that reduces them gives a positive test.","It needs a free aldehyde or a hemiacetal that can open to an aldehyde.","So a reducing sugar gives a positive test."],"others":"A negative test describes a non-reducing sugar. Reducing sugars are oxidised by bromine water. A sugar with no free carbonyl group, such as sucrose, is non-reducing.","trap":"Positive test = reducing sugar.","answer":"gives a positive test with Tollens' reagent or Fehling's solution"},"chem-12-14-m11":{"idea":"Acetic anhydride reacts with OH groups, so counting acetyl groups counts OH groups.","steps":["Each OH group takes up one acetyl group.","Glucose gives a pentaacetate: five acetyl groups.","So glucose has five OH groups.","Four are secondary and one is primary."],"others":"The product shows OH groups, not carbonyl groups, so five ketone or five aldehyde groups are wrong. The reaction does not count carbon atoms.","trap":"Penta = five.","answer":"five hydroxyl groups"},"chem-12-14-m12":{"idea":"Bromine water oxidises an aldehyde to a carboxylic acid and keeps the carbon chain.","steps":["Glucose gives gluconic acid with bromine water.","Gluconic acid has a COOH group at carbon 1.","Only an aldehyde group changes to COOH like this.","So glucose has an aldehyde group."],"others":"Glucose has no double bond and no ether link. A ketone would not give an acid with the chain kept, so fructose is not oxidised this way.","trap":"Bromine water separates aldoses from ketoses.","answer":"an aldehyde group, which is oxidised to a carboxylic acid group"},"chem-12-14-m13":{"idea":"Anomers differ only at the new chiral carbon made when the ring closes.","steps":["In ring form, C-1 comes from the aldehyde group.","The OH on C-1 can be below the ring (α) or above it (β).","The two forms differ only in configuration at C-1.","Both have the same formula and the same six carbons."],"others":"C-6 is the CH₂OH carbon and is the same in both forms. The formula is C₆H₁₂O₆ for both. Both have six carbons.","trap":"Anomeric carbon = C-1 in glucose.","answer":"the configuration at C-1, the carbon of the ring that came from the aldehyde group"},"chem-12-14-m14":{"idea":"Proteins are chains of amino acids joined by amide links.","steps":["The COOH of one amino acid reacts with the NH₂ of the next.","A water molecule is lost.","The link formed is –CO–NH–.","This link is called the peptide bond."],"others":"The glycosidic bond joins sugar units. The phosphodiester bond links nucleotides in nucleic acids. The ester bond –CO–O– is found in fats.","trap":"Peptide = proteins, glycosidic = sugars, phosphodiester = DNA.","answer":"the peptide bond, –CO–NH–"},"chem-12-14-m15":{"idea":"An amino acid has an acid group and a base group in one molecule, so they react with each other.","steps":["COOH gives a proton to NH₂ in the same molecule.","This forms +NH₃ and COO⁻.","The molecule has both charges, so it is a zwitterion.","These charges explain the high melting point and good solubility in water."],"others":"A single anion, a single cation, or a neutral NH₂ and COOH form is not the main form in neutral water. The charges are balanced inside one molecule.","trap":"Zwitterion = dipolar ion, net charge zero.","answer":"a zwitterion, with both +NH₃ and COO⁻ groups"},"chem-12-14-m16":{"idea":"At the isoelectric point the positive and negative charges are equal.","steps":["At this pH, the amount of the + form equals the − form.","So the net charge is zero.","With no net charge it does not move in an electric field.","Molecules do not repel one another, so they separate out most easily.","Solubility is therefore least."],"others":"A net positive or net negative charge is not the case at this point. Moving fastest needs a large charge, but here there is none. Solubility is least, not greatest.","trap":"Zero charge, zero movement, least solubility.","answer":"has no net charge, and its solubility in water is the least"},"chem-12-14-m17":{"idea":"Essential means the body needs it from food because it cannot make it.","steps":["The body makes some amino acids itself.","Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body.","They must be supplied in the diet.","Examples are valine, leucine and lysine."],"others":"The body does not make essential ones in large amounts. They all take part in making proteins. They occur in both plant and animal food.","trap":"Essential = must eat.","answer":"the body cannot make, so they must come from the food"},"chem-12-14-m18":{"idea":"Protein structure has four levels, and the primary level is the order of amino acids.","steps":["Primary structure is the sequence of amino acids.","The amino acids are joined by peptide bonds.","A helix or sheet is the secondary structure.","The folded 3D shape is the tertiary structure.","The joining of several chains is the quaternary structure."],"others":"The joining of chains is the quaternary level. The overall shape is tertiary. A helix or sheet is secondary.","trap":"Primary = order only.","answer":"the sequence of the amino acids in the chain"},"chem-12-14-m19":{"idea":"The α-helix is held in shape by weak hydrogen bonds.","steps":["The chain coils into a spiral.","Each N–H group forms a hydrogen bond with a C=O group.","That C=O is four residues away on the same chain.","Many such bonds hold the helix steady."],"others":"Ionic bonds between chains are not the main force in the helix. Covalent bonds between carbons do not give the coil. Peptide bonds are the links along the chain, not the helix-holding bonds.","trap":"In a β-sheet the hydrogen bonds join neighbouring chains.","answer":"hydrogen bonds between the C=O and N–H groups of the chain"},"chem-12-14-m20":{"idea":"Denaturation destroys the folded shape but not the order of amino acids.","steps":["Heat breaks the weak hydrogen bonds and other forces.","The secondary and tertiary structures are lost.","Peptide bonds are strong and stay.","So the primary structure stays the same.","The protein loses its activity, usually for good."],"others":"Heat does not change one amino acid into another, or make a carbohydrate. The peptide bonds are not broken, so the primary structure is not lost.","trap":"Boiled egg white does not turn liquid again.","answer":"the secondary and tertiary structures are lost, but the primary structure stays"},"chem-12-14-m21":{"idea":"Enzymes are biological catalysts, and they are proteins.","steps":["An enzyme speeds up a reaction in a living cell.","Almost all enzymes are proteins.","Each enzyme fits a particular substrate, like a key in a lock.","It is not used up in the reaction."],"others":"Enzymes are not lipids or carbohydrates. They are not nucleic acid templates, and they are specific, not general. They are not used up.","trap":"Enzyme = protein catalyst.","answer":"proteins that act as catalysts, and are specific for a reaction"},"chem-12-14-m22":{"idea":"DNA and RNA differ in their sugar, and the order in the question matters.","steps":["The DNA sugar is 2-deoxyribose, which has one oxygen less at C-2.","The RNA sugar is ribose.","Read the question in order: DNA first, then RNA.","So the answer is deoxyribose and ribose."],"others":"Glucose and fructose are not found in nucleic acids. The pair ribose and deoxyribose has the right sugars but in the wrong order.","trap":"Deoxy = without one oxygen, in DNA.","answer":"deoxyribose and ribose"},"chem-12-14-m23":{"idea":"A nucleotide has three parts: sugar, phosphate and base.","steps":["A nucleoside is a sugar joined to a nitrogen base.","Adding a phosphate group to it gives a nucleotide.","So a nucleotide = sugar + phosphate + base.","Nucleotides join to make the nucleic acid chain."],"others":"Fatty acids and amino acids are not part of a nucleotide. Each wrong choice also misses the phosphate group or the base.","trap":"Nucleoside + phosphate = nucleotide.","answer":"a sugar, a phosphate group and a nitrogen base"},"chem-12-14-m24":{"idea":"Bases pair in a fixed way in DNA.","steps":["Adenine pairs with thymine in DNA.","This pair has two hydrogen bonds.","Guanine pairs with cytosine by three.","So A–T has two bonds."],"others":"Cytosine pairs with guanine, not adenine, and guanine is not the partner of adenine. Thymine is right, but three hydrogen bonds is wrong for A–T.","trap":"A–T: 2 bonds. G–C: 3 bonds.","answer":"thymine (T), by two hydrogen bonds"},"chem-12-14-m25":{"idea":"RNA has uracil in place of thymine.","steps":["DNA bases: A, G, C and T.","RNA bases: A, G, C and U.","Uracil is found in RNA only.","So uracil is the answer."],"others":"Adenine and cytosine are in both DNA and RNA. Thymine is in DNA only, so it is the opposite of what the question asks.","trap":"U for RNA, T for DNA.","answer":"uracil"},"chem-12-14-m26":{"idea":"The order of amino acids in a peptide decides which compound it is.","steps":["In Gly–Ala, the COOH of glycine joins the NH₂ of alanine.","In Ala–Gly, the COOH of alanine joins the NH₂ of glycine.","The two joins are different.","So they are different compounds, even if the formula is the same."],"others":"The same amino acids do not mean the same compound. The same molecular formula only makes them isomers. Both have a peptide bond.","trap":"Peptides are read from the free NH₂ end.","answer":"different compounds, as the order of the amino acids is different"},"chem-12-14-n01":{"idea":"In a chain, each peptide bond joins two neighbouring residues.","steps":["n residues in a single chain have n − 1 peptide bonds.","Here n = 9.","Number of bonds = 9 − 1 = 8."],"trap":"Check: the first residue forms no bond, and each of the other 8 forms one.","answer":"8"},"chem-12-14-n02":{"idea":"The number of ordered arrangements with repeats is a simple product.","steps":["There are 3 positions in a tripeptide.","Each position can hold any of 20 amino acids.","Order matters, so Gly–Ala–Val differs from Val–Ala–Gly.","Number = 20 × 20 × 20 = 8000."],"trap":"Check: it is 20³, a count of ordered triples.","answer":"8000"},"chem-12-14-n03":{"idea":"Number of molecules = moles × Avogadro's constant.","steps":["Moles = mass / molar mass = 36 / 180 = 0.20 mol.","Molecules = 0.20 × 6.022 × 10²³.","= 1.2044 × 10²³, about 1.20 × 10²³.","In units of 10²³ the answer is 1.20."],"trap":"Check: 180 g/mol comes from 6 × 12 + 12 × 1 + 6 × 16.","answer":"1.20"},"chem-12-14-n04":{"idea":"Each peptide bond forms by losing one water molecule.","steps":["Three glycine molecules join with two peptide bonds.","Two water molecules (18 g/mol each) are lost.","M = 3 × 75 − 2 × 18.","= 225 − 36 = 189 g/mol."],"trap":"Check: C₆H₁₁N₃O₄ gives 72 + 11 + 42 + 64 = 189.","answer":"189 g/mol"},"chem-12-14-n05":{"idea":"In double-stranded DNA, A equals T and G equals C.","steps":["A = 28 percent, so T = 28 percent.","A + T = 56 percent.","G + C = 100 − 56 = 44 percent.","G = C, so G = 44 / 2 = 22 percent."],"trap":"Check: in 1000 bases, 280 A, 280 T, 220 G and 220 C.","answer":"22"},"chem-12-14-n06":{"idea":"Count hydrogen bonds pair by pair: 2 for A–T and 3 for G–C.","steps":["A–T pairs: 10 × 2 = 20 bonds.","G–C pairs: 15 × 3 = 45 bonds.","Total = 20 + 45 = 65 hydrogen bonds."],"trap":"G–C pairs are stronger because of the extra bond.","answer":"65"},"chem-12-14-n07":{"idea":"Use moles: one mole of sucrose gives one mole of glucose.","steps":["Moles of sucrose = 171 / 342 = 0.50 mol.","Sucrose + H₂O → glucose + fructose.","Moles of glucose = 0.50 mol.","Mass = 0.50 × 180 = 90.0 g."],"trap":"Check: products = 171 + 9 (water) = 180 g, and half of it is glucose.","answer":"90.0 g"},"chem-12-14-n08":{"idea":"Each chiral carbon can have two arrangements.","steps":["With n different chiral carbons there are 2ⁿ stereoisomers.","Here n = 4.","2⁴ = 16.","Eight are D sugars and eight are L sugars."],"trap":"Check: 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 16.","answer":"16"}};
